-It ships the current commit (`git archive HEAD`, so commit first), runs
-`remote/bootstrap.sh` on both hosts in parallel (build tools, Rust, the
-Bessemer wheel, FastAPI and uvicorn at the versions used in the same-host run,
-the Axum release binary, and `oha`), starts the agent with a fresh random
-token, runs the comparison from the client, and copies `raw.json` and
-`summary.md` back.
+The runner stages the comparison files from the release worktree and starts
+each attempt in its own remote directory. It installs the published Bessemer
+wheel and pinned FastAPI stack, builds Axum with its locked dependency graph,
+and installs the pinned `oha` client. It saves the raw data and Markdown
+summary in the local output directory.
 
 ### Google Cloud
 
-`remote/gcp.sh` creates the two VMs, runs the above through `gcloud compute
-ssh`, and deletes them:
+The first cloud comparison uses Ubuntu 24.04 on a `c3-standard-8` server with
+one thread per core and a separate `c3-highcpu-22` load generator. There are
+four usable server cores and eight billed server vCPUs. By default both VMs
+use internal addresses in `europe-west2-a` and a compact placement policy.
+The benchmark sends application traffic between the private IPs. [C3
+machine types](https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/general-purpose-machines#c3_series)
+and [SMT billing](https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/set-threads-per-core).
 
-    PROJECT=my-project benchmarks/compare/remote/gcp.sh all
+Use a dedicated pair in a subnet with outbound package access, and SSH
+through IAP or an existing private connection. The load generator needs
+TCP access to the benchmark agent on port 9100 and to the server ports
+assigned from the host's ephemeral range. Limit that access to the benchmark
+pair. The IAP path uses TCP 22 from the IAP forwarding range and requires
+the appropriate IAP and SSH access in the project.
 
-- Server: `c3-standard-16` with SMT off, so 8 physical cores and configs up
-  to `8-core` without two workers sharing a core.
-- Client: `c3-highcpu-44`, about five times the server's cores.
-- Both in `europe-west2-a` (override with `ZONE`) in one compact placement
-  group, talking over the default VPC's internal network.
-- About US$3 an hour on demand. A default run is three rounds of four
-  configs, three frameworks and four routes at 13 seconds each, so about 30
-  minutes plus 10-15 minutes of building.
+Run the short qualification on two existing VMs first.
 
-`gcp.sh up`, `run` and `down` do the steps separately, for example to rerun
-without rebuilding the VMs. `all` deletes the VMs even if the run fails.
+```sh
+PROJECT=my-project ZONE=europe-west2-a SERVER=bench-server CLIENT=bench-client \
+OUT_DIR="$PWD/gcp-smoke" benchmarks/compare/remote/gcp.sh run \
+  --configs 1-core --connections 64 --rounds 1 --warmup 1 --duration 3
+```
+
+To provision a fresh pair, run the full comparison and clean it up, use
+`all` with the benchmark project and subnet. Use a fresh `PREFIX` for
+each pair. The `up`, `run` and `down` actions are also available for a
+benchmark pair that needs more than one run.
+
+```sh
+PROJECT=my-project SUBNET=bench-subnet PREFIX=bessemer-rc10-bench \
+OUT_DIR="$PWD/gcp-rc10-c256" benchmarks/compare/remote/gcp.sh all
+```
+
+The default matrix tests 1, 2 and 4 cores at 256 connections. Bessemer
+1.0.0rc10 uses release-default admission and loop selection, FastAPI 0.165.0
+uses Uvicorn 0.54.0 with uvloop and httptools, and Axum 0.8.9 runs natively
+with the matching Tokio thread count. Python uses the same Pydantic version
+across both frameworks. The three common routes are small JSON, path and
+query parameters, and a 20-item typed JSON echo. Three rotated rounds of
+3-second warmup and 10-second measurement take about 18 minutes of load, plus
+startup and setup. Each result keeps successful throughput, latency, status and
+error distributions, CPU utilisation and response checks.
+
+Use distinct output directories for a 64- or 1,024-connection follow-up.
+Run the nominal 1 ms `/io` route separately with
+`--routes io --frameworks bessemer-auto,fastapi-standard`. The Python
+baseline starts on CPython 3.13.18. To compare standard and free-threaded
+Python 3.14, run separate passes with `PYTHON_VERSION=3.14.7` and
+`PYTHON_VERSION=3.14.7t`, keeping the same VM pair and connection counts.
 
 ### Why not GitHub Actions
 
